The balanced inverse-generated SAT core does not survive the required domain attack: across 36 linear regular planted instances (45, 90, and 135 variables; degrees 3–6), exact-one DPLL with unit propagation solved 36/36, never exceeding 64 branch nodes or 0.102 seconds. That rules out Track A for the paper-licensed hard construction. I also checked the paper’s Section 2 gadget route for Track B: one color-refinement round separates all 16 gadget vertices, so its 256 local inspections are essentially the same work as the “compact” reconstruction—there is no meaningful mechanical/insight gap. I’m documenting this as an H rejection rather than shipping an easy generator.
